// js-rt.metajs -- JAVASCRIPT'S (AND TYPESCRIPT'S) OWN SEMANTICS, in MetaJS: the layer 2
// runtime js-to-llvm-ir.abnf and typescript-to-llvm-ir.abnf link for a native build.
//
// ONE FILE FOR BOTH LANGUAGES. TypeScript's externs are a subset of JavaScript's, so
// typescript-to-llvm-ir.abnf links THIS module verbatim. There is no
// typescript-rt.metajs and there should not be one: the two grammars share abnf/jsrt.go
// + abnf/jsrtjsprint.go + abnf/jsrtjsbig.go on the Go side for exactly the same reason.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/js-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-js-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/js-rt.ll that both grammars link next to lib/runtime.ll. -rt-lib
// exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` below under its own C symbol, so nothing
// else in this file is visible to the linker - the helpers are deliberately named
// jv*/jx*/jb*.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrtjsprint.go (the js_js* value surface),
// abnf/jsrtjsbig.go (BigInt), abnf/jsrtregexjs.go plus abnf/jsrtregex.go (the js_jsx*
// regular expressions), and the js_has / js_defprop / js_iterable / js_supercall /
// js_supget / js_supset / js_call_new / js_this / js_newtarget block of abnf/jsrt.go.
// This file matches them function for function; where it CANNOT, the site says so in
// full. Written against the Go CODE, not the Go comments.
//
// ----- the extern split ------------------------------------------------------
//
//     the C floor            the generic primitives, js_truthy, js_genfn /
//                            js_yield / js_ctl_*, js_keys, js_scope_*
//     layer 2, NEUTRAL       js_has, js_defprop, js_iterable, js_supercall,
//                            js_supget, js_supset, js_call_new, js_this,
//                            js_newtarget
//     layer 2, JS's own      js_js* and js_jsx*
//
// Three of the js_js* exist ONLY because the shared name could not be changed:
// js_jskeys, js_jssupget and js_jssfield answer what js_keys / js_supget / js_set
// answer for everything except a CLASS DESCRIPTOR, whose object model js/ts alone
// has. The extern map is one map for every language, so a fixed behaviour that only
// js/ts should see needs a js-only name - manual chapter 4's js_jvsub / js_csmcall /
// js_ktsmcall rule.
//
// ----- what js/ts does NOT need ------------------------------------------------
//
//  1. ZERO `_raw` PARAMETERS. Every `handle(k)` in either grammar feeds js_num_i,
//     js_closure or js_arg, all three of them floor externs.
//  2. Generators cost this file NOTHING for the DRIVING half: both grammars emit
//     js_genfn(js_closure(...)) and then reach the generator through js_get(g, "next")
//     + js_call, which the floor's get_member answers with a bound gen_next. `yield`
//     is js_yield, a floor extern. What js DOES owe is the INSPECTION half -
//     `.return()` and `.throw()` - and the for-of drain (js_iterable).
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local (or a reassigned parameter) keeps the TYPE
// CLASS it is first assigned, so a variable that holds a number in one branch and an
// object in another is declared `var x = anytype`; there is no ASI, so every `return`
// carries its value on the same line and a value-less early return is BRACED; no
// for..in, no splice, no split/join/lastIndexOf, indexOf ignores its from-index,
// array.length is not assignable, and there are no exponent literals.

// ===================================================== the dynamic `this` ===
//
// `js_this` and `js_newtarget` read abnf/jsrt.go's thisStack / newTargetStack - the Go
// runtime's own CALL STACK, which the floor's js_call does not keep at all
// (runtime.c's js_call drops `self` for a tag-7 closure and hands jsdispatch only
// (env, args)). No floor extern answers it, so it is kept HERE, in layer 2, because
// layer 2 is a module with globals of its own and because every call that carries a
// receiver goes through a function in THIS file (js_jsmcall, js_supercall,
// js_call_new) or through a js_call site the emitter owns.
// The emitter's half is one extern call, emitted ONLY under -exe (see jsCall in
// js-to-llvm-ir.abnf): `js_jssetthis(receiver)` immediately before each js_call. No
// restore is needed and that is worth stating, because it looks like a bug: `this`
// is read exactly ONCE per function, at ENTRY, where the emitter binds it into the
// scope (`js_scope_decl(scope, "this", js_this())`). A nested call that overwrites
// the cell therefore cannot be observed by the caller, which already copied it.
//
// The module under llvm.Run does not use this: it still declares js_this/js_newtarget
// and abnf/jsrt.go answers them from its own stacks.

// `anytype` and not `undefined`: a MetaJS local pins the TYPE CLASS of its first
// assignment, and these two hold every value in the language.
var jvThis = anytype
var jvNewTarget = anytype

function js_jssetthis(v) {
    jvThis = v
    jvNewTarget = undefined
    return undefined
}

// js_jssetnew(ctor, receiver): the js_call_new arming, split out so that the emitter
// can use it directly for `new F()` and layer 2 for a super() chain.
function js_jssetnew(ctor, v) {
    jvThis = v
    jvNewTarget = ctor
    return undefined
}

function js_this() { return jvThis }

function js_newtarget() { return jvNewTarget }

// ===================================================== value predicates =====

function jvIsObjLike(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return true }
    return typeof v == "object"
}

function jvIsObj(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return false }
    return typeof v == "object"
}

// js_jsargs(args, off): the arguments object of a call, a copy of its argument array
// from index off (an instance method's receiver comes first) whose kind word is 19 in
// its low five bits and, above them, the value of its own `length` - the argument
// count, which an element write does not change. jvArgsN is set once one exists, so
// a program without one pays nothing on a `length` read. Twins: jsIsArgs / jsArgsLen
// in both *-interpreter.abnf, js_jsargs / jsvIsArgs in abnf/jsrtjsprint.go.
var jvArgsN = 0
function jvIsArgs(v) { return Array.isArray(v) && arrKind(v) % 32 == 19 }
function jvArgsLen(v) { return (arrKind(v) - 19) / 32 }
// An index of an arguments object is an own property when it is below `length` or
// holds a value, and what an iteration, a spread, apply and an Array method read is
// the list up to `length`. Twins: jsvArgsHas / jsvArgsView in abnf/jsrtjsprint.go,
// jsArgsHas / jsArgsView in both *-interpreter.abnf.
function jvArgsHas(v, k) {
    var n = jvToNumber(k)
    if (jvStr(n) !== jvStr(k)) { return false }
    if (n < 0) { return false }
    if (Math.floor(n) !== n) { return false }
    if (n >= v.length) { return false }
    if (n < jvArgsLen(v)) { return true }
    return v[n] !== undefined
}
function jvArgsView(v) {
    var out = []
    var n = jvArgsLen(v)
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        if (i < v.length) { out.push(v[i]) } else { out.push(undefined) }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}
function jvArgsKeys(v) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < v.length) {
        if (jvArgsHas(v, i + "")) { out.push(i + "") }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}
function js_jsargs(a, off) {
    var c = []
    for (var i = off; i < a.length; i++) { c.push(a[i]) }
    jvArgsN = 1
    return arrKindSet(c, 19 + 32 * c.length)
}

// The MAPPED arguments object of a sloppy function with plain parameters (ECMA-262
// 10.4.4.7). js_jsargmap(names, arguments) is the names mapped to arguments[0..n),
// the parameters below the argument count, which the call's scope keeps under
// "0argm". js_jsargsync(scope, name, v), after a store to a name, copies v into
// arguments when the binding the store reached is a mapped parameter; js_jsargput
// (scope, obj, k, v, unmap), after a store to obj[k], copies v into the parameter k
// maps to when obj is the arguments object of the call whose scope the chain reaches
// first (after a delete, unmap true, k stops being mapped). Twins: the same externs in abnf/jsrtjsprint.go, jsArgSync / jsArgPut in both
// *-interpreter.abnf.
function js_jsargmap(names, args) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < names.length) {
        if (i >= args.length) { return out }
        out.push(names[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}
function js_jsargsync(sc, name, v) {
    var s = anytype
    s = sc
    while (s !== undefined) {
        if (scopeHas(s, name)) {
            if (scopeHas(s, "0argm")) {
                var ns = scopeGet(s, "0argm")
                var ao = anytype
                ao = scopeGet(s, "arguments")
                var i = ns.length - 1
                while (i >= 0) {
                    if (ns[i] == name) {
                        if (jvIsArgs(ao)) {
                            if (i < ao.length) { ao[i] = v }
                        }
                        return undefined
                    }
                    i = i - 1
                }
            }
            return undefined
        }
        s = scopeParent(s)
    }
    return undefined
}
function js_jsargput(sc, obj, key, v, unmap) {
    if (!jvIsArgs(obj)) { return undefined }
    var s = anytype
    s = sc
    while (s !== undefined) {
        if (scopeHas(s, "arguments")) {
            if (scopeGet(s, "arguments") !== obj) { return undefined }
            if (scopeHas(s, "0argm")) {
                var ns = scopeGet(s, "0argm")
                var ix = jvToNumber(key)
                if (ix >= 0) {
                    if (ix < ns.length) {
                        if (jvStr(ix) === jvStr(key)) {
                            if (Math.floor(ix) === ix) {
                                if (ns[ix] !== "") {
                                    if (unmap) { ns[ix] = "" } else { scopeDecl(s, ns[ix], v) }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            return undefined
        }
        s = scopeParent(s)
    }
    return undefined
}

function jvIsCallable(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    return typeof v == "function"
}

// ===================================================== typeof, errors =======

// `typeof`. The emitter routes the user-visible `typeof` here in BOTH compiled
// halves (js-to-llvm-ir.abnf's typeofExt; the twin is js_jstypeof in
// abnf/jsrtjsprint.go): abnf/jsrtjsbig.go's BigInt is a Go TYPE whose js_typeof
// answers "bigint", and layer 2's is an ordinary object box that the floor's
// type_of can only call "object". Everything else is the floor's own answer,
// tag 16 ("function") included.
//
// A CLASS DESCRIPTOR IS A FUNCTION to `typeof`, and only to `typeof`. Every class
// here is an ordinary object carrying __isclass - which is what makes instanceof,
// `extends` and `new` plain object machinery - while node says "function" for every
// class. The test stays out of the engine's own `is this callable` guards on
// purpose: those decide whether a value can be CALLED, and a class descriptor still
// cannot.
function js_jstypeof(v) {
    if (jbIsBig(v)) { return "bigint" }
    if (jvIsObj(v)) {
        if (v["__isclass"] === true) { return "function" }
        if (v["__symkey"] !== undefined) { return "symbol" }
    }
    return typeof v
}

// A BigInt operation's TypeError / SyntaxError / RangeError is a JAVASCRIPT
// exception, not an engine abort: node runs the catch clause of
// `try { xs[0] + xs[1] } catch (e) { ... }` when xs holds a BigInt and a number.
// `fail` is the floor's abort and a js_try deliberately does not recover it;
// MetaJS's own `throw` compiles to the floor's js_throw, which is exactly what the
// emitted try/catch recovers. The value is a real Error INSTANCE whenever the
// emitter registered the hierarchy (jvErrFromText finds the class the message text
// already names), so e.message and `e instanceof TypeError` answer; `"" + e` is the
// message text either way. The Go twin's half of this is abnf/jsrtjsbig.go's
// bigRaise.
function jbRaise(msg) { throw jvErrFromText(msg) }

// ================================ the floor externs a BigInt is taken from ===
//
// Under -exe the emitter routes `===`, `!==`, unary `-`, `!`, `typeof` and
// compile-core's truthy() here (see seqExt / sneExt / negExt / notExt / typeofExt /
// core.truthyExt in js-to-llvm-ir.abnf). Every one of them exists because
// abnf/jsrt.go's BigInt is a Go TYPE with an arm in strictEq / truthy / js_neg /
// typeOf, and layer 2's is an ordinary object box the C floor would answer
// "truthy", "identity", NaN and "object" for. Each delegates to the floor's own
// operator for every value that is not a BigInt, so a program without one pays a
// call and nothing else.

function js_jsseq(a, b) {
    if (jbIsBig(a)) {
        if (jbIsBig(b)) { return jbCmp(a, b) == 0 }
        return false
    }
    if (jbIsBig(b)) { return false }
    return a === b
}

function js_jssne(a, b) { return !js_jsseq(a, b) }

function js_jsneg(v) {
    if (jbIsBig(v)) { return jbNeg(v) }
    if (jvSymOn) { jvSymOperand(v, undefined) }
    return -jvToNumber(v)   // NEVER `0 - v`: the signed-zero trap of part 3.
}

function js_jsnot(v) {
    if (jbIsBig(v)) { return !jbTruthy(v) }
    return !v
}

// The `__raw` SUFFIX: -rt-lib exports this as js_jstruthy and puts the result
// through the floor's own js_truthy, which is the raw 1/0 compile-core's
// `icmp ne ..., 0` compares. Answering the HANDLE of a MetaJS boolean would answer
// 2 for false, and 2 != 0, so every condition in the program would be taken.
function js_jstruthy__raw(v) {
    if (jbIsBig(v)) { return jbTruthy(v) }
    return v
}

// js_scope_set_or_create is the C floor's own (runtime.c, next to js_pyset_var), so
// this file must NOT define a twin: a top-level `function js_*` here is an exported C
// symbol and would be a redefinition at link time.

// ##########################################################################
// # LAYER 2, PART A - the JavaScript VALUE SURFACE (abnf/jsrtjsprint.go)
// ##########################################################################

// ===========================================================================
// jsrt-value.metajs -- JavaScript's VALUE SURFACE in MetaJS.
//
// The Go twin, function for function, is abnf/jsrtjsprint.go plus the
// js_has / js_defprop / js_iterable / js_supercall / js_supget / js_supset /
// js_call_new block of abnf/jsrt.go. Written against the Go CODE.
//
// Helper prefix: jv. Every top-level `function js_*` below is an exported C symbol.
//
// ----- what layer 2 has to supply that the Go runtime got for free ----------
//
//  1. There is NO js_mcall in the C floor (runtime.c exports js_get/js_set and a
//     small bound-method table, nothing that dispatches a method on an instance).
//     abnf/jsrt.go's js_jsmcall falls back to js_mcall -> rt.memberCall; here that
//     fallback is written out at the end of js_jsmcall (jvFallbackCall).
//  2. DELETE is the floor's js_del, reachable from MetaJS as the host builtin `delKey`
//     (runtime.c host id 63, abnf/jsrtint.go, metajs-interpreter.abnf), so key ORDER
//     after delete-then-reinsert, for-in and object spread are the floor's answers.
//  3. An ACCESSOR is a Go type (*jsAccessor) there and an object box here:
//     {__acc: 1, g: getter, s: setter}. js_jsmget / js_jsmset / js_supget /
//     js_supset / js_has are the five places that know the box.
//  4. accessorCount is jvAccN: while no accessor has ever been defined, js_jsmget
//     and js_jsmset are a straight delegation to js_get / js_set, which matters
//     because the emitter routes EVERY member and index read through js_jsmget.
//
// The MetaJS subset rules apply throughout (docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md): no ASI,
// a local keeps the type class it is first assigned, no for..in, no splice/split/
// join/lastIndexOf of our own, array.length is not assignable, no exponent literals.

// ===================================================== small utilities =====

// The "this method / this receiver is not ours" sentinel, the shape jxMiss has.
// A UNIQUE object, so no user value can ever be mistaken for it.
var jvMiss = {}

// The CALL-SITE MISS SENTINEL. `xs[1]()` with undefined in the slot is a catchable
// TypeError in node, whose message is the callee's SOURCE TEXT (`xs[1] is not a
// function`), which no runtime value carries - `xs[1]` and `xs[i]` arrive as the
// same key - so the split is: this file says only "nothing claimed the call" by
// answering the sentinel, and the CALL SITE, which knows the text at compile time,
// raises. The emitter makes the object and hands it over here in its boot block,
// so both engines compare the very same handle. Empty until then, and while it is
// empty jvFallbackCall aborts.
var jvMissCall = anytype
var jvMissCallSet = false
function js_rt_misscall(v) { jvMissCall = v; jvMissCallSet = true }

// The two RAW PREDICATES the js/ts call site branches on, twins of js_jsfnp /
// js_jsctorp in abnf/jsrtjsprint.go. The `__raw` suffix (manual 7.1) is what makes
// -rt-lib export them returning a plain 0/1 instead of a boolean HANDLE, which is
// what the emitter's icmp needs.
function js_jsfnp__raw(v) {
    if (jvIsCallable(v)) { return 1 }
    return 0
}
function js_jsctorp__raw(v) {
    if (jvIsObj(v)) {
        if (v["__isclass"] !== undefined) { return 1 }
    }
    return 0
}

// The accessor-property count. abnf/jsrt.go's rt.accessorCount: while it is 0 the
// member paths cannot meet an accessor and skip the whole lookup.
var jvAccN = 0
// jvClsN is the same idea as jvAccN one line up, for the STATIC NAMESPACE: while no
// class with a static table has been built, every member read skips the
// class-descriptor test outright (js_jsmget is hot; see its head comment).
// js_jsstatics, which the emitter calls once per class definition, is what bumps it.
var jvClsN = 0

// 1 once any Error instance exists; see js_jserrinit.
var jvErrN = 0

// The program's Math object and the names of its builtin members, which are NOT
// ENUMERABLE in node. A floor object lists every key it holds, so jvErrHide drops
// these by name; js_jsdel forgets a deleted one, so a delete-then-write makes it an
// ordinary enumerable property. The Go twin keeps them out of the key order instead.
var jvMathObj = anytype
var jvMathHid = anytype
var jvJsonObj = anytype

// The `String` global js_jsstringfn handed out, kept so that js_jsmcall can
// recognize the receiver of String.fromCharCode (the Go twin tests hf.name).
var jvStringFn = anytype

// The `Array` global js_jsarrayfn handed out, for Array.isArray and instanceof.
var jvArrayFn = anytype

// The `Number` global js_jsnumberfn handed out, for Number.prototype.
var jvNumberFn = anytype

// The `BigInt` global js_jsbigintfn handed out, for BigInt.prototype.
var jvBigIntFn = anytype

// The `Boolean` and `Function` globals js_jsbooleanfn and js_jsfunctionfn handed out.
var jvBooleanFn = anytype
var jvFunctionFn = anytype

// The count of prototype views (jvProtoView): while it is 0 no member write can
// meet one, and js_jsmset skips the test.
var jvPvN = 0

// SYMBOLS. A symbol is an object {__symdesc, __symkey}: a new one per Symbol() call,
// one per name for the well-known ones and per key for Symbol.for. As a property KEY
// it is its __symkey - "__#@" and a name no identifier can spell - so every key
// listing already skips it, as it skips the engine's own "__" slots, and the
// private-name exemption files a class method under it. Every member extern that
// takes a key turns a symbol into its key first, once jvSymN says one exists. Twins:
// the symbol block of abnf/jsrtjsprint.go, jsSym* in both *-interpreter.abnf.
var jvSymN = 0
var jvSymWK = {}
var jvSymReg = {}
// Every symbol by "$" + its key, for Object.getOwnPropertySymbols.
var jvSymByKey = {}
var jvSymbolFn = anytype
// jvSymN > 0 as a boolean, for the operators' symbol test. The arithmetic ones ask it
// only once jbStrict is armed, which the first symbol also does.
var jvSymOn = false
function jvSymMake(desc, key) {
    var s = {}
    s["__symdesc"] = desc
    s["__symkey"] = key
    jvSymByKey["$" + key] = s
    jvSymN = jvSymN + 1
    jvSymOn = true
    jbStrict = true
    return s
}
function jvIsSym(v) {
    if (!jvIsObj(v)) { return false }
    return v["__symkey"] !== undefined
}
function jvSymStr(s) {
    var d = anytype
    d = s["__symdesc"]
    if (d === undefined) { return "Symbol()" }
    return "Symbol(" + d + ")"
}
function jvSymWellKnown(n) {
    var s = anytype
    s = jvSymWK[n]
    if (s !== undefined) { return s }
    s = jvSymMake("Symbol." + n, "__#@" + n)
    jvSymWK[n] = s
    return s
}
// The well-known symbols, the static members of Symbol. Twins: jsSymWKNames in
// js-interpreter.abnf, jsvSymWKNames in abnf/jsrtjsprint.go.
function jvSymIsWK(n) {
    return n == "asyncIterator" || n == "hasInstance" || n == "isConcatSpreadable" || n == "iterator" ||
        n == "match" || n == "matchAll" || n == "replace" || n == "search" || n == "species" ||
        n == "split" || n == "toPrimitive" || n == "toStringTag" || n == "unscopables"
}
// ToString / ToNumber of a symbol throw: `"" + s`, `${s}` and `s * 1` where String(s)
// and s.toString() answer "Symbol(desc)". Twins: jsvSymConvErr in abnf/jsrtjsprint.go,
// jsSymConvErr in js-interpreter.abnf.
function jvSymConvErr(toStr) {
    if (toStr) { throw jvErrFromText("TypeError: Cannot convert a Symbol value to a string") }
    throw jvErrFromText("TypeError: Cannot convert a Symbol value to a number")
}
// ToNumber's TypeError when either operand is a symbol. The callers test jvSymOn
// first, so a program that makes no symbol never calls it.
function jvSymOperand(a, b) {
    if (jvIsSym(a) || jvIsSym(b)) { jvSymConvErr(false) }
}
// The own symbol-keyed properties of an object, listed in its __symkeys in the order
// they were first written: the floor's key listing skips every "__" key, and object
// spread and Object.assign still copy them. Twin: jsSymTrack in js-interpreter.abnf
// (the Go twin reads them off the key order).
function jvIsSymKey(k) {
    if (typeof k != "string") { return false }
    if (k.length < 4) { return false }
    return k.substring(0, 4) == "__#@"
}
function jvSymTrack(o, k) {
    var ks = anytype
    ks = o["__symkeys"]
    if (ks === undefined) {
        ks = []
        o["__symkeys"] = ks
    }
    if (ks.indexOf(k) < 0) { ks.push(k) }
}
function jvSymKeys(o) {
    if (!jvIsObj(o)) { return [] }
    var ks = anytype
    ks = o["__symkeys"]
    if (ks === undefined) { return [] }
    return ks.slice()
}
function jvSymUntrack(o, k) {
    var ks = anytype
    ks = o["__symkeys"]
    if (ks === undefined) { return undefined }
    var keep = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < ks.length) {
        if (ks[i] !== k) { keep.push(ks[i]) }
        i = i + 1
    }
    o["__symkeys"] = keep
    return undefined
}
// A string's CODE POINTS: a surrogate pair is one, a lone surrogate one of its own.
function jvCodePoints(o) {
    var cps = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < o.length) {
        var c = o.charCodeAt(i)
        var w = 1
        if (c >= 55296 && c <= 56319 && i + 1 < o.length) {
            var t = o.charCodeAt(i + 1)
            if (t >= 56320 && t <= 57343) { w = 2 }
        }
        cps.push(o.substring(i, i + w))
        i = i + w
    }
    return cps
}
// The iterator [Symbol.iterator](), values(), keys() and entries() answer over an
// array or a string: kind 1 yields the elements - a string's CODE POINTS - kind 0 the
// indices and kind 2 [index, element] pairs. An array is read live. Its own
// [Symbol.iterator] answers itself. Twins: jsvSeqIter in abnf/jsrtjsprint.go,
// jsSeqIter in js-interpreter.abnf.
